Send the CP what Android sends it, in Android's order

With the IMS context and the whole carrier-configuration exchange matched to
this modem's own Android byte for byte, the CP still refused every PDN but the
one it attached with. The 983-byte SETUP_DATA_CALL differs in zero bytes --
replaying Android's own captured frame draws cause 2253 while Android's draws
cause 0, cid 2, active 1 -- so no field of it is wrong.

What differs is everything around it. Over the first 22 seconds of a boot
Android puts 419 requests on a control stack, spanning 79 message ids. This
plugin put 89, spanning 27. Of Android's requests, 183 are ids this plugin
never sends at all. The CP builds its state from that traffic, and matching
one request inside it was never going to be enough.

So reproduce it. mm-sit-boot-parity.h is generated from /proc/sitdump captured
on komodo across a cold boot: Android's requests on one stack up to its first
IMS PDN, less the ones this plugin already sends and less the subsystems it
drives its own way -- SMS, SIM, STK and calls. 124 requests, 41 ids, verbatim
payloads at the offset the CP reads them from. Two rows name a subscription
rather than a constant, so SET_OPEN_CARRIER_INFO takes the SIM's MCCMNC and
SET_MOBILE_DATA_STATE the logical stack at run time. They go out in two phases,
where Android sends them: before the radio, with the rest of the boot
configuration, and after it while the CP is searching.

The IMS sequence is reordered to match as well. Android brings its AIMS stack
up three seconds before it asks for the IMS context and only registers the PDN
afterwards; this plugin asked for the context first, which was its own
invention. STEP_PDN now skips itself when a context is already in hand, so a
retry still keeps the one it has.

Also corrected here: MISC_CARRIER_CONFIG_STATUS reporting zero was read as a
failure, and it is not. Zero means the CP already holds that manifest and had
nothing to fetch, which is what it reports on every boot after the first.
Handed a hash it does not have it opens <dir>/manifests/<hash> over RFS within
a second, and handed a different valid one it fetches that manifest and all of
its confseqs -- 13 files, the same ones Android fetches.

--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/plugins/samsung-sit/mm-sit-boot-parity.h
@@ -0,0 +1,256 @@
+/* SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later */
